What the licence change does and does not do
============================================

Cairn was released under the MIT License from its first commit through v0.5.1.
From the release following that tag it is licensed under the Sustainable Use
License, in the file LICENSE beside this one.

A licence change is not retroactive, and nobody should read it as one.

  - Every release up to and including v0.5.1 remains MIT, permanently, for
    anyone who already has it or obtains it later. That cannot be withdrawn.
  - The git history remains MIT. A fork taken from any commit up to that tag
    carries the MIT terms with it.
  - Only new work, from the next release onward, is under the new licence.

Why
---

Cairn is going to have a hosted offering alongside the code you can run
yourself. MIT permits anyone to take this software and sell exactly that hosted
service, including in competition with the people who wrote it, and that is a
poor foundation to fund the work from.

The Sustainable Use License keeps what matters about an open project: you can
read the source, run it yourself for your own business or personally, modify it,
fork it, and share those changes. What it stops is one specific thing — selling
Cairn itself as a service.

This is the licence n8n publishes and uses, and the change is made for the same
reason they made it.

Contributions
-------------

Contributions made before this change were made under MIT and are acknowledged
as such. Contributions from this point are made under the Sustainable Use
License; CONTRIBUTING.md says so.

If you contributed to Cairn under MIT and would rather your work were not
relicensed, say so in an issue and it will be honoured.
